[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) was merged as **one issue, one fix queued**. The reports merged into
it show three different problems. Engineering closed #4471 on 5 October, and that closed two of
them. The third is still open.

## Where each problem stands

| problem | engineering, 5 Oct | ticket |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `parser` | fixed: `fix(dates): parse dd/mm for eu locales` | [#1](https://github.com/sobierajpawel/support-kb/issues/1) closed, completed |
| `hint` | won't fix: booking-v3 form retired | [#2](https://github.com/sobierajpawel/support-kb/issues/2) closed, not planned |
| `one-day` | not addressed | [#3](https://github.com/sobierajpawel/support-kb/issues/3) **open** |

<Callout kind="warn">
#4471 is closed, but the one-day problem is not fixed. The parser fix does not touch it, because
those dates are read correctly and then land a day early. Send new one-day reports to #3, not to
#4471.
</Callout>

## What the reports show

<ProblemTable ticket={4471} repo="sobierajpawel/support-kb" issues={{ parser: 1, hint: 2, 'one-day': 3 }} />

Counts are unique reports. The export lists some reports twice, so its raw row count is higher.

## The three problems

**`parser`: the shared parser reads every date month first.** Day-first tenants (all eleven on
file) type `07/04` for 7 April and get 4 July. This is the problem #4471 describes, and the
fix shipped on 5 October.

**`hint`: booking-v3 prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field.** People at day-first tenants on
booking-v3 type what the hint asks, month first, and the form reads it day first. The symptom is
the same flipped date. The cause is the hint, so the parser fix does not touch it. It comes only
through the form channel of booking-v3 tenants.

**`one-day`: the date is right, but the event lands a day early.** No day and month are swapped.
One client sees Tuesday, another sees Monday for the same meeting; an invite arrives "before" the
booking. That points at a time zone conversion, not at date parsing. The desk already read these
as one-day moves (`desk-notes.json`), but the incident page still counts them under `parser`.

## Why one ticket hid three

- Merging is by subject line ([decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject)). All three
  problems produce the same subjects: "wrong day", "date is wrong".
- Intake tags each report from the reporter's browser. The cause depends on the tenant's locale,
  form and channel, so the tags disagree with the cause (see the tag column on the
  [#4471 page](/kb/incidents/4471)).
- The #4471 table counts by cause and leaves the one-day moves inside `parser` as a footnote.

## What to do

1. Keep one ticket per problem. #3 stays open until the one-day reports stop.
2. Close a ticket when engineering resolves its own problem, not when the merged ticket closes.
3. Split by tenant, form and channel, not by subject, when a big ticket grows.

